this is modern-day reindeer herding  Sweden's indigenous Sami people crossing  frozen lakes on snowmobiles to feed the  reindeer by hand a symptom of their  disappearing postures here in the Arctic  reindeer herding is so important for the  Sami culture in in Sweden we are  struggling to just getting the reindeer  to survive every winter and it's very  costly and put a lot of strains and  families and reindeer herders the  effects of climate change and the impact  of mining and dams are part of a growing  list of problems adding to that strain  these reindeer have been moved up into  the mountains for the spring and summer  where despite all the dangers that face  them they should at least be safe from a  new one that emerged over the winter  that new danger was a spate of killings  of reindeer suspected to have been  carried out by Swedish neighbors in  retaliation for a court case that a Sami  community one against the government and  which gave them back the right to decide  who hunts and fishes on their land  lash Ola Yannick is one of those who has  found his animals slaughtered in the  woods I lit my reindeer loose here and a  man drove up and said you don't come  here with your reindeer or will shoot  them we've already shot seven or eight  and if I meet you alone in the forest  I'll shoot you too it's been a tense  winter most of us Swedes are good people  but we have this clique that has such  hatred towards reindeer and reindeer  herders tensions rose in January after  Sweden's Supreme Court ruled in favor of  the Sami reindeer herding union of  Guinea's whose land stretches some 300  kilometres from here near the Norwegian  border it could set a precedent for 50  other such areas covering half of Sweden  and may also hinder plans for more wind  and water power plants and mines which  have already decimated reindeer pastures  the government in Stockholm has  condemned what it described as racism  threats and hate against the Sami but  hasn't commented on any strengthening of  their rights we've got some evidence  the Supreme Court stated that the  international indigenous rights  conventions Sweden is bound by must be  observed in decisions effect in the Sami  we must see changes in legislation and  see it applied not least when it comes  to mining and forestry back up north the  case has caused uncertainty both for  Swedish hunters who now need permissions  from the reindeer herders Union and for  Sami who don't have membership tempering  enthusiasm for what is a rare victory  for their people  and while the coronavirus devastates the  economy the creation of yet more  industry on these lands could be a  bigger priority than giving the Sami a  real say in the destiny of the country  Paul Reis al Jazeera vice alotta Sweden
 

